# Any file whose bytes are hashed as an input to conversion, calibration, or a
# determinism gate MUST carry an explicit eol=lf attribute. A bare `text=auto`
# checks out CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux, so a SHA-256 over the file differs
# by platform — a hash that certifies nothing in a formula whose entire job is
# cross-platform identity. (SuperSLM_Plan.md §11, bench 2026-07-14.)
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
tests/golden/**  -text
*.sslm  binary

# Source: normalized to LF in the repo, tool-native on checkout.
*.h    text
*.cpp  text
*.md   text

# The vendored spike reference (tests/reference/superslm_spike/*.py) is hashed by
# check_provenance.py -- a determinism gate -- so per the policy above it must be
# eol=lf, not tool-native. A bare `text` checks out CRLF on Windows and the pin
# (computed on Linux/LF) fails there. (S-HARDEN-5 provenance, bench 2026-07-24.)
*.py   text eol=lf

# cmd.exe batch files mis-seek around multi-line parenthesized blocks when the
# working copy's line endings are LF-only (a tool-written LF copy bypasses the
# checkout smudge core.autocrlf=true would otherwise apply) -- the interpreter
# eats the first character of a subsequent command and the script fails with
# spurious "'x' is not recognized" errors. Pinned to CRLF explicitly rather than
# left to core.autocrlf so a tool-written LF working copy cannot recur.
# (Claude/Bach/t2139-battery-varsunset-failure-2026-08-16.md, Wizard repo.)
*.bat  text eol=crlf
